		<h2>Net mitigation rate derivation</h2>
		<p>
			The rate applied in the presentation, 748 lbs CO2e/MWh, is not taken from a source. It is the
			output of the calculation below. It is specific to ground-mounted solar with battery storage
			built on cleared forest land, and it should not be read as a general figure for Massachusetts
			solar. Siting on rooftops, parking canopies or brownfields carries no forest penalty and would
			produce a higher rate.
		</p>
		<p>
			Start with generation. A direct PV footprint acre generates 400 MWh a year on a dc basis, which
			converts to ac output at the 1.31 dc/ac ratio. Each footprint acre carries 1.667 cleared acres,
			so a cleared acre produces roughly 184 MWh a year and 4,601 MWh over 25 years before
			adjustments. Degradation reaching 13.55% by 2050 brings that to 4,228 MWh. Curtailment rising
			from zero to 10% brings it to about 4,020 MWh.
		</p>
		<p>
			At 1,014 lbs CO2e/MWh, those 4,020 MWh avoid 1,849 MT CO2e per cleared acre. Four adjustments
			follow.
		</p>
		<div class="overflow-x-auto">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Component</th>
						<th>MT CO2e per cleared acre</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td>Gross avoided grid emissions</td><td>1,849</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Less solar and BESS embodied carbon</td><td>-184.5</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Less existing forest carbon stock released</td><td>-283</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Less 25 years of foregone forest sequestration</td><td>-37.5</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Plus long-duration wood product credit</td><td>+19.8</td></tr>
					<tr><td><strong>Net 25-year mitigation</strong></td><td><strong>1,364</strong></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			Dividing 1,364 MT CO2e by 4,020 MWh gives 0.339 MT, or 748 lbs, of CO2e avoided per
			megawatt-hour. That is 74% of the 1,014 lbs gross rate.
		</p>

		<h2>Shortfall scenario and results</h2>
		<p>
			The scenario runs the CECP solar trajectory against a case in which Massachusetts installs 80%
			of it each year from 2026 through 2050. Planned capacity comes from the Massachusetts
			Department of Energy Resources <em>Workbook of Energy Modeling Results</em> (December 2022),
			Phased Scenario, Tab 8 for electric capacity and Tab 10 for generation, interpolated between
			the milestone years 2025, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2045 and 2050. The recent installation trend comes
			from the Solar Energy Industries Association <em>Massachusetts Solar State Fact Sheet</em>,
			read off the published graphic because SEIA does not publish the underlying table, and
			converted from MWdc to MWac at 1.31. The ISO New England comparison comes from <a
				href="https://www.iso-ne.com/static-assets/documents/100022/2025_final_pv_forecast.pdf"
				>Final 2025 Photovoltaic Forecast</a
			>, prepared for the 2026 CELT report, April 29, 2025, p. 12.
		</p>
		<p>
			The missing capacity accumulates year over year. Each year's foregone generation is the
			cumulative missing capacity run at that year's CECP capacity factor. Over 25 years the
			shortfall totals 116 TWh of lost clean electricity. At 748 lbs CO2e/MWh that is about 39.5
			million metric tons of unbudgeted CO2e.
		</p>
		<p>
			The forest acreage figure divides those emissions by 301 MT CO2e per acre, which is the 283 MT
			of carbon stock an acre holds plus 37.5 MT of sequestration over 25 years, less the 19.8 MT
			wood product credit. That gives roughly 131,000 acres. Readers should note what this divisor
			counts. Most of it is carbon already standing in the forest rather than carbon the forest
			removes from the air over the study period. On sequestration flow alone, at 1.5 MT per acre
			per year for 25 years, offsetting 39.5 million metric tons would take about 1.05 million acres.
		</p>
		<p>
			The carbon budget figures come from the same CECP workbook. The roughly 800 million metric ton
			all-sector budget is the sum of the milestone-year limits with the intervening years
			interpolated, which totals 795 MMT. The 147 MMT electric-sector budget comes from the workbook
			directly. The bathtub framing follows Climate Interactive and the work of John D. Sterman at
			MIT Sloan.
		</p>

